# The Winpthreads Library

This library provides POSIX threading APIs for mingw-w64.

## Supported Windows Versions

The `winpthreads` library can run on any Windows NT system starting with
Windows NT 3.1; see configuration details below.

When configured with Win9x support, `winpthreads` can be loaded on all Win9x
systems, however it is not functional on Win9x in its current state.

Support for Win9x is in progress.

### Windows NT

You can use `--with-winnt=version` option to specify the minimal Windows NT
version you want `winpthreads` to be able to run on. The supported values are:

| Value    | Meaning         |
|----------|-----------------|
| nt3.1    | Windows NT 3.1  |
| nt3.5    | Windows NT 3.5  |
| nt3.51   | Windows NT 3.51 |
| nt4.0    | Windows NT 4.0  |
| win2000  | Windows 2000    |
| winxp    | Windows XP      |
| winvista | Windows Vista   |
| win7     | Windows 7       |
| win8     | Windows 8       |
| win8.1   | Windows 8.1     |
| win10    | Windows 10      |

Default and allowed values for `--with-winnt` option are target-dependent:

| Target            | Minimal Allowed Value | Default Value                             |
|-------------------|-----------------------|-------------------------------------------|
| x86               | `nt3.1`               | `nt3.1` with mingw-w64; `winxp` with MSVC |
| x64               | `winxp`               | `winxp`                                   |
| arm32             | `win8`                | `win8`                                    |
| arm64 and arm64ec | `win10`               | `win10`                                   |

### Win9x

You can define `WINPTHREADS_WIN9X` macro to non-zero value in order to build
`winpthreads` with Win9x support.  
This macro is only meaningful for x86 and is ignored on other targets.

This macro can be defined to non-zero value only when value passed to
`--with-winnt` option is `winxp` or lower.

By default, it is enabled with mingw-w64 (if the above condition is true) and
disabled with MSVC.

## Linking Against Winpthreads

For maximum compatibility, winpthreads headers expose APIs without the
`dllimport` attribute by default.

If your program is linked against the DLL, you may define the macro
`WINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT` to add `dllimport` attributes to all APIs,
which makes function calls to them a bit more efficient.

Be careful when you build static libraries; you probably do not want to define
`WINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT` unconditionally, to ensure that your code can link
against either shared or static winpthreads. Note that winpthreads does not
export any variables, which means that `dllimport` is not requred, with both
MSVC and mingw-w64 toolchains.
